1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,350
Section 4

2
00:00:01,350 --> 00:00:07,070
Last week, we started looking at reptiles, including crocodiles and snakes.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:11,199
Today, I'd like us to have a look at another reptile - the lizard -

4
00:00:11,199 --> 00:00:15,289
and in particular, at some studies that have been done on a particular type

5
00:00:15,289 --> 00:00:20,140
of lizard whose Latin name is tiliqua rugosa.

6
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:23,372
This is commonly known as the sleepy lizard,

7
00:00:23,372 --> 00:00:29,174
because it's quite slow in its movements and spends quite a lot of its time dozing under rocks

8
00:00:29,174 --> 00:00:30,880
or lying in the sun.

9
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,840
I'll start with a general description.

10
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:39,260
Sleepy lizards live in Western and South Australia, where they're quite common.

11
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,365
Unlike European lizards,

12
00:00:41,365 --> 00:00:46,797
which are mostly small, green and fast-moving, sleepy lizards are brown,

13
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:51,047
but what's particularly distinctive about them is the colour of their tongue,

14
00:00:51,047 --> 00:00:55,978
which is dark blue, in contrast with the lining of their mouth which is

15
00:00:55,978 --> 00:00:57,000
bright pink.

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,410
And they're much bigger than most European lizards.

17
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:05,692
They have quite a varied diet, including insects and even small animals,

18
00:01:05,692 --> 00:01:10,190
but they mostly eat plants of varying kinds.

19
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:15,688
Even though they're quite large and powerful, with strong jaws that can crush beetles

20
00:01:15,688 --> 00:01:19,380
and snail shells, they still have quite a few predators.

21
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:24,776
Large birds like cassowaries were one of the main ones in the past,

22
00:01:24,776 --> 00:01:29,390
but nowadays they're more likely to be caught and killed by snakes.

23
00:01:29,390 --> 00:01:33,881
Actually, another threat to their survival isn't a predator at all,

24
00:01:33,881 --> 00:01:39,033
but is man-made - quite a large number of sleepy lizards are killed by

25
00:01:39,033 --> 00:01:41,880
cars when they're trying to cross highways.

26
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:48,023
One study carried out by Michael Freake at Flinders University investigated the methods of

27
00:01:48,023 --> 00:01:49,910
navigation of these lizards.

28
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:54,840
Though they move slowly, they can travel quite long distances.

29
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:59,109
And he found that even if they were taken some distance away from their

30
00:01:59,109 --> 00:02:03,058
home territory, they could usually find their way back home as long as they

31
00:02:03,058 --> 00:02:08,330
could see the sky - they didn't need any other landmarks on the ground.

32
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:13,293
Observations of these lizards in the wild have also revealed that their mating habits

33
00:02:13,293 --> 00:02:14,880
are quite unusual.

34
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:21,344
Unlike most animals, it seems that they're relatively monogamous, returning to the same partner

35
00:02:21,344 --> 00:02:22,910
year after year.

36
00:02:22,910 --> 00:02:28,538
And the male and female also stay together for a long time, both before

37
00:02:28,538 --> 00:02:31,050
and after the birth of their young.

38
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:35,230
It's quite interesting to think about the possible reasons for this.

39
00:02:35,230 --> 00:02:39,749
It could be that it's to do with protecting their young - you'd expect

40
00:02:39,749 --> 00:02:43,345
them to have a much better chance of survival if they have both parents

41
00:02:43,345 --> 00:02:44,610
around.

42
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:49,327
But in fact observers have noted that once the babies have hatched out of

43
00:02:49,327 --> 00:02:53,160
their eggs, they have hardly any contact with their parents.

44
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,950
So, there's not really any evidence to support that idea.

45
00:02:56,950 --> 00:03:03,640
Another suggestion's based on the observation that male lizards in monogamous relationships tend to be bigger

46
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:05,890
and stronger than other males.

47
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:10,518
So maybe the male lizards stay around so they can give the female lizards

48
00:03:10,518 --> 00:03:12,610
protection from other males.

49
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:15,430
But again, we're not really sure.

50
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:20,976
Finally, I'd like to mention another study that involved collecting data by tracking the

51
00:03:20,976 --> 00:03:21,820
lizards.

52
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:24,990
I was actually involved in this myself.

53
00:03:24,990 --> 00:03:29,770
So we caught some lizards in the wild and we developed a tiny GPS

54
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,979
system that would allow us to track them,

55
00:03:31,979 --> 00:03:34,730
and we fixed this onto their tails.

56
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:37,076
Then we set the lizards free again,

57
00:03:37,076 --> 00:03:39,802
and we were able to track them for twelve days

58
00:03:39,802 --> 00:03:43,271
and gather data, not just about their location,

59
00:03:43,271 --> 00:03:47,160
but even about how many steps they took during this period.

60
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,399
One surprising thing we discovered from this is that there were far fewer meetings

61
00:03:52,399 --> 00:03:57,075
between lizards than we expected - it seems that they were actually trying to

62
00:03:57,075 --> 00:03:58,440
avoid one another.

63
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,700
So why would that be?

64
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,886
Well, again we have no clear evidence,

65
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:09,176
but one hypothesis is that male lizards can cause quite serious injuries to one

66
00:04:09,176 --> 00:04:09,497
another,

67
00:04:09,497 --> 00:04:15,308
so maybe this avoidance is a way of preventing this 一 of self-preservation, if

68
00:04:15,308 --> 00:04:16,150
you like.

69
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:19,834
But we need to collect a lot more data before we can be sure

70
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:20,740
of any of this.
